The most basic condition for hallucinations is incorrectness, whether measured against a source [ 45 , 117 ] , the real-world facts [ 82 , 3 , 2 ] , or an instruction [ 39 , 12 ] . Incorrect outputs, however, are not uniform in how they arise or how they respond to elicitation. Accordingly, the difference that matters most in practice is consistency of occurrence. Yet some incorrect responses can be corrected under a semantically equivalent phrasing ( semantic illusion ), while others recur whatever phrasing is used ( confabulation ) (see § D for an explanation of the terminology). We ask whether these reflect distinct error types, and whether that distinction changes which detection and mitigation strategies apply. The difference also invites an epistemic reading, since a response that becomes correct under a semantically equivalent phrasing shows the relevant knowledge is accessible without changing model weights or injecting additional information. We therefore treat knowledge as behaviorally accessible when some tested condition elicits a correct response. The conditions we admit that establish accessibility are semantically equivalent phrasings, instruction variants, decoding configurations (e.g., temperature, nucleus sampling), which require no access to model internals and add no additional hints or context. Notation. Let 𝒱 ⁡ ( Q ) \mathcal{V}(Q) be a set of semantically equivalent variants of question Q Q sharing the same ground truth but introducing no additional hints. For each variant v ∈ 𝒱 ⁡ ( Q ) v\in\mathcal{V}(Q) , r ⁡ ( v ) r(v) denotes the model response; correct ⁡ ( r ⁡ ( v ) ) = 1 \mathrm{correct}(r(v))=1 if it matches the ground truth, abstain ⁡ ( r ⁡ ( v ) ) = 1 \mathrm{abstain}(r(v))=1 if the model expresses uncertainty (confidence score = 0 =0 or explicit refusal). Definition 1 Confabulation (CF) A response to question Q Q is a confabulation if the model produces an incorrect, non-abstaining answer on every variant in 𝒱 ⁡ ( Q ) \mathcal{V}(Q) 